<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Run settings for MTM_Waitlist.Tests.

  Why this file exists: without a timeout a test that never finishes takes the whole run with it. The run then
  looks like it never started (no summary, no failure, nothing named), and the test host it leaves behind keeps a
  lock on the test assembly, so every later run waits on that lock and looks stuck too. That is what a "test run
  that never starts" is, seen from the outside.

  The two settings below are the documented way to bound that (Microsoft Learn, "Configure unit tests with a
  .runsettings file"):

    RunConfiguration/TestSessionTimeout  the whole session is terminated after this many milliseconds
    MSTest/TestTimeout                    a single test case is given this many milliseconds

  The values are deliberately generous. Every unit test in this suite is milliseconds, and the slowest legitimate
  ones are a few seconds (a bounded connect to an unreachable host, a priming step that waits three seconds). A
  test that reaches the limit is a hang rather than a slow test, and the runner then names it instead of waiting
  for ever.

  tools/run-tests.ps1 passes this file explicitly and adds the blame hang detector, so a run started from the
  command line is protected by both the timeout and the detector.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <!-- Fifteen minutes for the whole assembly: the suite takes well under two, so this only ever trips on a hang. -->
    <TestSessionTimeout>900000</TestSessionTimeout>
  </RunConfiguration>

  <MSTest>
    <!-- Ninety seconds for one test. A test that needs longer than this is not measuring anything worth waiting for. -->
    <TestTimeout>90000</TestTimeout>
    <CaptureTraceOutput>true</CaptureTraceOutput>
  </MSTest>
</RunSettings>
